{
  "0": "For all four datasets, change only interaction scoring to h^T diag(1+0.1*tanh(a))e, with jointly trained a initialized to zero. This preserves the initial dot product while allowing bounded dimension-specific compatibility, which may improve ranking without destabilizing normalized embeddings. Keep the causal backbone, inputs, readout, loss and optimizer fixed; mirror scoring in the predictor writer. Train one model per category.",
  "1": "For all four datasets, change only readout to a masked recency-weighted mean of the last three nonpadding causal encoder states, with weights 1, 2, 4 from oldest to newest, renormalized for shorter histories. This may reduce last-state noise while retaining recent intent, particularly in Electronics. Preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer; use identical readout during training and prediction. Train one model per category.",
  "2": "For all four datasets, change only embedding normalization: use effective item embeddings e/max(1,||e||2/0.75), tightening the previously successful norm cap from 1 to 0.75. Apply consistently to input and candidate lookups, retain zero padding and existing tying and dimensions. Stronger norm control may improve generalization in weaker categories. Preserve backbone, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; mirror normalization in the predictor writer. Train one model per category.",
  "3": "For all four datasets, change only causal context length to max(10,floor(0.75*L)), where L is the starting program's maximum history length, retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order. Removing the oldest quarter may reduce stale-interest interference with modest computational savings. Preserve position-encoding convention, feature definitions, embedding design, backbone, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Match truncation in the predictor writer; train one model per category."
}
